    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d349">

                <pb facs="0287" n="275" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg275"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111A.R.–N.C./24: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Nicholson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-01-31T13:00:00-06:00">January 31, 1940—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3:02 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9. Department’s circular telegram January 27, 3 p.m. The Acting Foreign
                        Minister<anchor n="31" xml:id="d349-f1"/> states that thus far no question
                    in this regard has been received but that in the event his Minister at
                    Washington asks, Nicaragua’s attitude will agree with that of Brazil and the
                    United States.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nicholson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d349-f1" n="31" xml:id="d349-f1-content">
                <p>Antonio Barquero.</p>
